  "summary": "trimprompt 1.0.42: heavily obfuscated npm package with child_process + HTTP POST exfiltration pattern",
  "details": "The trimprompt package ships almost every JavaScript file as heavily obfuscated single-line bundles using hex-identifier renaming (_0x50c8b1, _0x4c1680, etc.) across 20+ files including cache-manager.js, ccr.js, dashboard.js, executor.js, file-watcher.js, hooks/claude-hook.js, mcp.js, proxy-conv.js, proxy-resp.js, redactor.js, sync.js, tracker.js, and language filters (devops/git/go/js/python/rust/shell). sync.js contains the co-occurrence of require('child_process'), an HTTP POST, and 'hostname' identifiers consistent with system reconnaissance and outbound transmission. shims.js at line 67 imports child_process and invokes execSync('pwsh...') for PowerShell execution. The combination of pervasive identifier-renaming obfuscation across the entire package, child_process usage bound with HTTP POST and host-identifier collection in sync.js, and PowerShell execSync in shims.js diverges materially from the transparency expected of a prompt-trimming utility. Obfuscation on this scale hides the destination and payload of the network calls from inspection.",
